WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:07.760
Here we have the PDB entry 3WU2. This is a&nbsp;
structure of the Photosystem II complex that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:13.440
plays a pivotal role in photosynthetic light&nbsp;
capture and water splitting. In this tutorial,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:18.080
we will explore this structure using some of the&nbsp;
MolStar (Mol*) Viewer's animation capabilities.

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:25.920
Under Export Animation, there are a&nbsp;
number of animation modes to choose from.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:31.120
The one we will explore right now is&nbsp;
Camera Spin. The default parameters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:37.920
for duration and speed will produce one 360º&nbsp;
rotation that takes 4 seconds to complete.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:43.840
For now, let's proceed with these&nbsp;
settings to create our first animation.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:49.360
Before we hit Render though, we need to&nbsp;
adjust the resolution under the Camera icon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:55.120
For this tutorial, I'll be going&nbsp;
with dimensions of 1280×720,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:59.760
which are good dimensions for uploading&nbsp;
to YouTube. Now you may have noticed that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:06.160
the resolution displayed in the animation panel&nbsp;
does not match the resolution set above. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:12.480
because we have Auto-crop turned on. Once&nbsp;
we turn it off, the resolutions match up.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:21.840
Now we can render the animation and wait for&nbsp;
it to export. With the rendering complete,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:26.800
we can hit Save Animation to receive&nbsp;
the file into the Downloads folder.

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:30.400
And here is the finished animation.

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:42.160
Returning to the Mol* Viewer, we will now cover&nbsp;
the customization of the Camera Spin parameters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:48.880
Let's say we want the structure to complete&nbsp;
two full 360º rotations instead of just one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:56.560
To do this, we will adjust the Speed slider to&nbsp;
2. However, notice that the duration is still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:02.240
at the original 4 seconds. This means that the&nbsp;
spin cycles are probably going to be too fast.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:07.440
To solve this problem, we can set&nbsp;
the Duration slider to 8 seconds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:11.520
preserving the default ratio&nbsp;
of 1 rotation per 4 seconds.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:17.840
In your own projects, feel free to experiment&nbsp;
with different ratios to create animations of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:23.440
varying rotational speed. For this tutorial,&nbsp;
I'll move ahead with the current settings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:28.320
by clicking Render, and downloading&nbsp;
the file once the export is complete.

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:36.400
Now the structure makes two full&nbsp;
rotations, but at the same speed as before.

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:47.200
It is also possible in the Mol* Viewer to&nbsp;
animate specific parts of a protein structure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:52.320
In this next animation, we will visualize&nbsp;
Photosystem II's active site, which is where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:58.880
the water splitting reaction occurs. To do this,&nbsp;
we start by unrolling this drop-down menu and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:06.640
selecting Item 20 — the calcium-manganese-oxygen&nbsp;
cluster. Then, upon clicking OEX as shown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:12.640
a rendering of the cluster appears. To&nbsp;
make this depiction a little clearer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:17.360
we can go under Components and hide the&nbsp;
cartoon ribbon by clicking the "eye"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:24.000
icon. If we want, we can also remove some of&nbsp;
these interactions by going to these bar controls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:31.920
selecting Non-covalent Interactions,&nbsp;
and turning off the hydrogen bonds.

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:36.800
Once we land on a good orientation for the&nbsp;
active site, we can optimize the animation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:41.120
parameters. There is a lot of detail&nbsp;
to take in here, so we should choose a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:47.200
moderate pace for the rotation — something&nbsp;
like 7 seconds for the full 360 degrees.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:51.760
As before, we hit Render and&nbsp;
download the resulting file.

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:59.840
And here is the completed animation.

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:09.840
Finally, I will demonstrate another&nbsp;
animation capability that the Mol*&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:16.400
Viewer has to offer — Spin Structure. Instead&nbsp;
of producing a simple rotation around the y-axis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:20.400
(like Camera Spin), this mode will&nbsp;
turn the structure end-over-end.

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:26.880
The default duration is a bit fast, so&nbsp;
we will extend the slider to 5 seconds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:31.200
As before, we click Render, and&nbsp;
then download the exported file.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:41.840
And here is the finished animation.

